                <pb facs="0143" n="95" sameAs="frus:frus1877/pg95"/>

                <head>No. 68.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Seward to Mr. Evarts.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Legation of the United States</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Peking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1877-04-30"><hi rendition="#italic">April</hi> 30, 1877</date>.
                        (Received June 18.)</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 230.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to hand to you herewith
                    Mr. Consul Lord’s report on Wên-Chow, a port opened lately under the provisions
                    of the Chefoo convention, and my letter of advice to him thereupon.</p>
                <p>You will observe that I have stated that it would not be consistent with our past
                    policy to accept an exclusive grant of land at the port in question, and that,
                    while there is no occasion to make haste in appointing a consular agent, a
                    person of another nationality may be chosen, if a suitable American cannot be
                    found.</p>
                <p>I have, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer><signed>GEORGE F. SEWARD.</signed></closer>





